-- title:
--   The discrete kernel and the measurability of cone-domination sets
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$. For a kernel $k$ on $\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d$ with values in $[0,\infty]$ and a mesh $h$, the **discrete kernel** is the average of $k$ over the product of the two open cubes of side $h$ centred at the two arguments:
--
--   $$\omega^k_h(x,y) \;=\; h^{-2d} \int_{A_h(x)\times A_h(y)} k(s,t)\ d(s,t).$$
--
--   It is a kernel on the lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$, and the surrounding argument checks that it inherits the two-sided bounds required of a discrete kernel — for the upper bound, because on the product of the two cubes $\|s-t\|$ is bounded below by $\|x-y\|/(2\sqrt d)$, so the jump kernel there is at most $(2\sqrt d)^{d+\alpha}$ times its value at $(x,y)$.
--
--   The module also isolates the measurability hypothesis this requires. A configuration $\Gamma$ satisfies **the cone-domination measurability condition** when
--
--   $$\{x \in \mathbb{R}^d \ :\ V \subseteq \Gamma(x)\} \quad\text{is measurable for every subset } V \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d .$$
--
--   The source derives this from condition (M) by quoting a measurable-selection theorem of Debreu; that implication is not formalised, so the conclusion is carried as an explicit hypothesis wherever it is needed.
--
--   The discrete kernel is what lets a continuous comparison be attacked through a discrete one: a kernel on $\mathbb{R}^d$ is replaced by its cube averages, the discrete theorem is applied on the lattice, and the mesh is then sent to zero. The measurability condition is the one place where the qualitative hypothesis on $\Gamma$ has to be converted into something an integral can use, and naming it explicitly keeps the dependency on an unformalised external theorem visible.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The integral is a lower Lebesgue integral valued in $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{\infty}$ and requires no measurability of $k$ to be written down; the prefactor is `ENNReal.ofReal ((h ^ (2*d))⁻¹)`, which is $0$ for $h \le 0$, so the definition carries a junk value there. The condition quantifies over *all* subsets $V$, not merely double cones, so it is formally stronger than the property the source actually needs; it is also strictly stronger than the source's condition (M) itself, which is measurability of $\{(x,y) : y-x \in \Gamma(x)\}$. The file comment records that the estimate is proved directly at scale $h$ rather than transported along $x\mapsto hx$, the powers of $h$ cancelling exactly against the two cube volumes, so the constant is the same as at scale $1$.
